MASTER-SETUP.md: This file contains the global environment definitions (e.g. OS dependencies, service layout) and bootstrap commands. It is loaded first at startup to configure the session.
ADAPTOID-ENGINE.md: Describes the Project Intent + Problem Adapter mechanism. It defines how an agent should interpret PROJECT-INTENT.md, detect potential failure modes, and dynamically construct the plan with the right checks.
PROJECT-INTENT.md: This is the human-provided mission statement, success criteria, domain context, and constraints for the current project. It replaces ad-hoc prompts. For example, it should enumerate objectives, deliverables, and business rules. The agent uses this as authoritative guidance.
VERIFICATION-PROTOCOLS.md: Defines all multi-layer checks, audit trails, and guardrails (see below) that the agent must apply. This includes rules for structured output formats, grounded citations, test execution, and explicit fail states.
MEMORY/: Implements a multi-tier persistent memory (inspired by). Every session, the agent follows a fixed startup sequence (e.g. as shown in an AGENTS.md startup script): it loads identity and rules files (e.g. project intent), reads the most recent daily logs (DAILY-LOGS/today.md and yesterday.md), and then reads MEMORY.md. The DAILY-LOGS folder stores raw session transcripts (layer 1); an automated “heartbeat” process regularly distills those into the curated MEMORY.md (layer 2). A deeper Knowledge Base folder (layer 3) holds stable reference content (guides, models, long-term profiles) that is accessed on demand rather than every run. This design ensures true persistence: “If it’s not in a file, it doesn’t exist after restart”. It mirrors proven patterns where an agent reads memory files at boot to retain context. Markdown is used for all memory (not vectors or DBs) because it is human-readable, editable, versionable, and free.
Every agent session must include an explicit reload of these files (forced by ADAPTOID-ENGINE.md instructions). For example, the startup sequence could be:

Read MASTER-SETUP.md and ADAPTOID-ENGINE.md for global rules.
Read PROJECT-INTENT.md.
Read recent MEMORY/YYYY-MM-DD-*.md logs (today & yesterday).
Read VERIFICATION-PROTOCOLS.md.
Load SKILLS/ definitions.
Read curated MEMORY.md.
By codifying and automating this “context bootstrap” (as advocated by others), Adaptoid eliminates forgetting and context drift. The agent always starts with full awareness of project goals, its own identity and rules, and relevant history.

Adaptoid Engine: Project Intent & Problem Adapter
At the core is the Adaptoid Engine, which reads the project intent and adapts the entire pipeline to it. Unlike generic agents, Adaptoid includes a built-in Intent-Adapter that examines the goal and proactively injects the correct scaffolding. For example:

Understand and formalize goals: The Engine parses PROJECT-INTENT.md to extract objectives, scope boundaries, success metrics, and domain details. It translates these into structured subtasks (e.g. requirement specifications, planning steps). This echoes spec-driven AI methodologies.
Identify failure modes: It cross-references known pitfalls for the domain or task type (e.g. common hallucinations in legal summaries, dataset bias issues, lost context in coding) and appends mitigations to the plan. For instance, if hallucination risk is high, it adds explicit “verify facts” steps. If complexity is large, it enforces decomposition checkpoints. This is akin to providing phase-level grounding hooks as in Spec Kit Agents.
Context-Grounded Planning: Before executing any step, the Engine triggers discovery hooks that gather necessary context (repo structure, data schema, external APIs) to inform planning. After each major milestone it schedules validation hooks (e.g. run tests, sanity-check outputs).
Tool & Agent Selection: Based on intent, Adaptoid picks an optimal set of specialized agents/skills from its library. For complex, branching tasks, it may use a graph-based orchestrator (like LangGraph) to spawn and coordinate multiple worker agents. The Engine also chooses models (local or cloud) and adjusts settings (temperature, context window) according to the task difficulty and cost constraints.
This adapter layer makes every project setup unique and bulletproof. In practice, giving an AI the DevKit plus PROJECT-INTENT.md immediately yields a detailed step-by-step plan with integrated safeguards. This follows the principle of “reasoning before acting”. For example, after specification and planning phases, the Engine ensures that each implementation task is automatically followed by a test suite or linter pass (as shown in spec-driven examples). By externalizing intent and emergent problems into files and structured prompts, Adaptoid converts vague goals into concrete, verifiable workflows.

Verification-First, Anti-Hallucination Protocols
Adaptoid enforces multi-layered verification throughout the agentic pipeline, making errors expensive and detectable early:

Multi-Agent Validation Pipelines: Critical tasks use an “executor→validator→critic” pattern. One agent executes the action (e.g. fetch data, write a module), a second independently checks for consistency with the request, and an optional third renders final “APPROVED/REJECTED” verdict. This cross-check catches cases where a lone agent might hallucinate a plausible but wrong answer. For example, if a hotel booking agent tries to book an invalid hotel, the validator catches the discrepancy and flags failure.
Structured Outputs & Pass/Fail: All critical outputs use formal schemas or token markers. Instead of free-form text, the agent is instructed to output JSON or YAML with explicit status codes. Any deviation triggers a validation failure in the workflow (e.g. the runtime only proceeds if "status":"COMPLETED" is returned). This ensures silent failures are never silent – a missing or malformed field makes the check fail. The DevKit’s templates enforce these formats.
Evidence Grounding & Source Tracking: Agents must cite facts from authoritative sources or the workspace. For factual queries, retrieved information is sourced (e.g. Wikipedia with URL, database entries, or local knowledge base references). Any content not backed by a source is flagged. This echoes best practices to “anchor responses in fact” to reduce hallucination.
Test and Simulation Validation: For code or decision tasks, each subtask is followed by built-in checks: unit tests, type checks, linters, or rule-based validators. As in spec-driven engineering, after code generation the DevKit automatically compiles/runs it and verifies outputs. Failed tests immediately cause a rollback or rerun with adjusted prompts. This addresses the “black-box” failure by having a deterministic check at each step.
Human-in-the-Loop Gates: For any “go/no-go” decision (e.g. major design choices, high-impact changes), the workflow pauses and sends a review prompt to a human operator or an expert agent acting as approver. This explicit gate means no major step completes without approval. It adds accountability.
Memory & Configuration Audits: Before acting, the agent re-loads and cross-checks its memory and config files against expected invariants. For example, if MASTER-SETUP.md says “use Python 3.11,” the agent double-checks the environment. If a memory entry seems conflicting, it is verified with the user.
These layers combine structured planning with runtime checks. As shown in AWS’s Strands example, multi-agent validation makes the system explicitly mark “FAILED” on inconsistencies, instead of quietly outputting wrong answers. Adaptoid builds on this by adding neurosymbolic guardrails: rules enforced outside the LLM (e.g. regex checks on inputs, numerical bounds enforced by external code, access control checks). The result is a harsh cost for hallucination: every deviation generates a traceable error rather than creeping into outputs.

Best-in-Class OS/Harness Setup
The Adaptoid DevKit includes a production-grade deployment stack for Linux (Ubuntu) plus guidance for macOS/Windows variants (e.g. instructions for Docker Desktop on Mac/Win or WSL2 usage). The core is a Docker Compose orchestration with the following services:

Agent API (FastAPI): A Python FastAPI service serves as the main agent orchestrator. It exposes REST endpoints (e.g. /run-task, /status) to submit goals. FastAPI’s async handling allows concurrent tool calls (e.g. awaiting web queries, DB reads) without blocking. Pydantic schemas enforce request/response validation at the boundary.
LLM Runtime & Controllers: Containers provide the LLM engine(s). By default, we include a local LLM (e.g. a quantized 13B model running in Ollama or LM Studio) and an API gateway to cloud LLMs. The DevKit auto-detects GPU availability and routes heavy work to the GPU-enabled LLM container. A dynamic runtime (like LM Studio’s REST API) can load models on demand.
State Stores: A Redis cache stores ephemeral session state and flags. A PostgreSQL database archives completed tasks, conversation history, and audit logs. These allow agents to resume after interruptions and provide compliance-grade audit trails. Use of containers means each component can scale independently and recover via Docker health checks.
Vector/Knowledge DB: A specialized knowledge store (e.g. Chroma or Weaviate) holds embeddings for retrieval and similarity search. This accelerates context lookups and caching of previous computations (see Efficiency & Caching below).
Tooling & Skills Container: A utility container bundles command-line tools (curl, jq, git), Python libraries, and other utilities. Custom skills (in the SKILLS/ folder) are mounted into this container. This sandbox prevents the AI from accessing the host OS directly.
Networking & Sandboxing: By default the stack runs in a private Docker network, isolating it from the host. Outgoing requests (web fetch, external APIs) can be routed through a proxy container for logging. GPU access is enabled via Docker runtime options (--gpus).
The entire stack is defined in a root-level docker-compose.yml (and corresponding Dockerfile/Dockerfile.windows for cross-OS). The README provides a one-command launch (docker-compose up -d) and health-check command (docker ps). For Windows or Mac without Docker support, we include a script to deploy to Azure Container Instances or to run via Docker Desktop. The setup enforces strict versioning: everything is pinned (python env, OS packages, model versions). Configuration files in CONFIG/ manage secrets (GitHub tokens, API keys) via Docker env files.

This harness embodies best practices from modern AI stacks. FastAPI’s architecture with async tools support makes concurrent tool use trivial. Dependency injection handles shared resources safely. Docker orchestration and auto-restart policies provide self-healing: any failed container auto-restarts. We also include optional monitoring (e.g. a Grafana dashboard container) that pings the agent’s /health endpoints (demonstrated above). Editor configs (e.g. a VS Code workspace file) preinstall useful extensions (code linters, GitLens, Python AI assistants). The goal is that a developer or AI engineer can immediately open the cloned repo and, following the README, have a sandboxed AI-ready environment with GPU support and needed tools.

Curated High-Signal Skills Library
Adaptoid ships with a core skills library (in SKILLS/) that covers common agent tasks. Skills are organized by category, each encapsulated as a reusable template or code module (following the emerging Agent Skills specification):

System & OS Skills: File system navigation, shell command execution, process control, scheduling. (E.g. “save this data to /DATA”, “run a background process”, “monitor system health”.)
Data Retrieval & Processing: Web search (via APIs), database queries (SQL/NoSQL), file parsing (CSV/JSON/XML), data cleaning. (For example, an agent can fetch a CSV via HTTP and use Pandas to extract columns.)
Web & API Interaction: Authenticated API calls (e.g. GitHub, Google Sheets, Slack), browser automation (for tasks requiring a UI).
Programming & Code: Code generation and analysis skills in Python, JS, etc. Includes template prompts for generating unit tests, debugging, code refactoring. Also code-run capabilities (via a secure sandbox or local interpreter).
Knowledge & Documentation: Ability to query the KNOWLEDGE-BASE/ (e.g. search engineering docs), and summarization skills. (We provide a template that retrieves relevant MD files and asks the agent to focus on facts only.)
Math & Logic: Numerical computation (via Python / NumPy skill), logic puzzles, unit conversion, date/time calculations.
Communication: Email drafting and sending (via SMTP/Outlook plugins), chat/sms (via Twilio/Slack APIs), presentation / markdown report generation.
