Adaptoid-OS/
├── MASTER-SETUP.md        # System overview, software requirements, environment vars
├── ADAPTOID-ENGINE.md     # Core orchestration logic, default strategies, components
├── PROJECT-INTENT.md      # Parsed project goals, constraints, KPIs (adapted per project)
├── MEMORY-INDEX.md        # Curated session memory (distilled logs and facts)
├── VERIFICATION-PROTOCOLS.md  # Security policies and checklists to apply
├── skills/                # Core skill templates (YAML/JSON) by category
├── workflows/             # Declarative workflow templates (YAML graphs)
├── adapter/               # Project-Intent Adapter prompts, schemas, examples
├── examples/              # Sample project demos with context and results
└── references/            # Full bibliography and notes on patterns used
The agent’s startup hook forces re-loading these files before any new conversation (just as OpenClaw did with its AGENT.md and memory files). In each session, Adaptoid verifies the freshness of PROJECT-INTENT.md against the latest brief and the cache in MEMORY-INDEX.md, auto-refreshing if stale.
Memory Layers: Like Hermes, we use a pipeline: Session logs are stored in /logs/YYYY-MM-DD*.md. Periodic “heartbeat” prompts cause the agent to distill those logs into MEMORY-INDEX.md, retaining only enduring facts (name, projects, decisions) as recommended. Significant insights then bubble into an Obsidian-style vault (under obsidian/) for infrequently-needed reference. No external vector DB is required for core memory – we use simple Markdown and SQLite full-text search. This matches OpenClaw’s low-infra approach: “No external DB, no complex infra”.
Skills and Data: The /skills folder holds reusable skill definitions (in AgentSkills format) for tasks like web search, spreadsheet analysis, code execution, etc. Agents auto-load allowed skills each session (filtering by role and ACL). This is akin to CrewClaw’s 5700+ skill ecosystem. Because Adaptoid is local-first, this folder grows over time as new skills are created or adopted (the Hermes-style learning loop automatically saves new skills from repeated workflows).
ADAPTOID-ENGINE.md (Core Logic)
Adaptoid’s core is a framework-agnostic orchestrator plus the “Project Intent Adapter”. It does not rely on any single vendor. The Adaptive Core continually learns the best combination of patterns for the current domain:

Hybrid Architecture: We maintain an independent decision engine (Python CLI + config-driven) that integrates top frameworks via adapters. For example, we generate LangGraph-compatible YAML when a project needs complex DAG logic, or fall back to an AutoGen conversation when tasks are ill-defined. We include adapters for LangGraph, LangFlow, DeerFlow, MissionControl, Miyabi, Nimbalyst, etc. The core itself is framework-neutral, choosing which to invoke based on the parsed intent.

Project Intent + Problem Adapter: At the heart is an LLM-driven adapter. Given the raw project description, it produces:

Executable Configs (first): A complete set of YAML/JSON definitions: e.g. an .agf.yaml Agent Format spec, a full workflow graph for Conductor, MCP connector list, model routing rules, memory policy configuration, etc. These configs are directly machine-readable and enforceable.
Human Guidance (second): A Markdown action plan explaining decisions: steps, rationale, checkpoints. This is for the human overseer to review.
For example, feeding “Build a cross-platform weather analysis bot” yields an Agent Format spec (listing user inputs/output schema), MCP connectors (for weather API), a Conductor YAML decomposing tasks (parallel agent pipelines for data collection, analysis, report), and a verification section (tests for data accuracy).

The Adapter uses advanced prompting and the latest structured output techniques: it imposes JSON/YAML schemas so the LLM generates valid specs. (We can even re-use the Agent Format JSON schema to validate the output.)

VERIFICATION-PROTOCOLS.md
Adaptoid enforces “Trust but Verify” at runtime. Key protocols:

Declarative Policies: We declare machine-enforceable policies (allowlists/denylists on tools, file paths, APIs) as code. For example, a policy might forbid any agent from calling rm -rf or accessing /etc without human approval. These policies are written in YAML or JSON and version-controlled.
Enforcement Layers: The harness wraps all tool calls with interceptors. Before an agent’s action (API call, browser navigation, code execution), the engine checks policies. Unsafe actions are blocked or escalated. (This follows the “nope” design: if an action violates rules, it’s stopped.)
Structured Outputs: Every agent step outputs a strictly defined schema. We use JSON Schema/Pydantic to define expected outputs for each tool invocation or LLM response. The system catches any deviations immediately, retrying or raising errors instead of silently hallucinating bad data.
Checkpoints & Rollback: Long workflows are broken into checkpoints. After each major phase, Adaptoid runs automated tests or cross-agent reviews. For example, after a code-generation step, the orchestrator runs a syntax checker or unit tests (via a script step) before proceeding. If failures occur, it can roll back or loop in an “observer” agent to correct issues.
Audit Trail: All events are logged (by default to references/logs/). We integrate tracing (OpenTelemetry support in Graphiti/MCP connectors) so you can replay exactly what each agent did. The web dashboard visualizes the DAG with status, prompts, token counts, and policy violations (inspired by Conductor’s dashboard).
Human-in-the-Loop Gates: For high-risk steps, workflows include mandatory human review gates (with CLI/web prompts). This ensures compliance even when fully automated runs are possible. Gate steps are first-class in the YAML (see Conductor “Human gate” feature).
By layering these defenses, Adaptoid makes hallucinations, shortcuts, and policy violations visible and costly. The system treats hallucination-checks and schema validation as part of the normal loop, not an afterthought.

