/workflows/ – Workflow Templates
We include parameterized workflow templates for common scenarios. These are YAML graphs compatible with Conductor-style execution. Examples:

Code Review Pipeline (code-review.yaml): Agents: author, reviewer. The author agent generates code, then routes to reviewer. A script step runs tests; if tests fail, it loops back to author with test logs. Finally, merges to master on success. (See Conductor “script steps” and “routes” features.)

Research & Synthesis (research.yaml): Entry planner decomposes research goals. Then a parallel block {web_researcher, expert_scholar, data_analyst} gather info concurrently (failure modes: continue_on_error or all_or_nothing). Their outputs are templated into a synthesizer agent, which writes a report. Checkpoint: the synthesizer output is schema-validated and reviewed by a senior_researcher agent before completion.

Parallel Skill Tuning (parallel-exploration.yaml): Runs multiple tuning sessions in parallel (e.g. different hyperparams). Each agent invokes an external script or training job, writing results to separate folders. A final aggregator agent reads all results and picks the best.

Long-Horizon Task (long-term.yaml): Models a multi-day project with daily checkpoints. It uses time-based triggers (cron style steps) and heartbeat feedback (e.g. at end of each day, agent updates MEMORY-INDEX.md). Fail-safes include alerting a human if no progress for X hours.

Each workflow example is fully commented in /workflows, illustrating how to use conditionals (when clauses), human gate steps (type: human_interaction), and retries. We also provide a workflow registry concept: teams can publish workflows internally, and Adaptoid can fetch them by short name (similar to Conductor’s registries).

/adapter/ – Project Intent Adapter
This folder contains the logic and prompt templates for the Adaptoid Adapter – the “brain” that turns a project brief into actionable configs. It includes:

Adapter Prompt: A templated system prompt that instructs the LLM to output a JSON/YAML specification. For example:
less
Copy
# Adapter System Prompt
You are Adaptoid’s project intent parser. Given a project description, output two things:
(1) A YAML config (`project_config.yml`) with keys: workflows, agents, tools, memory_policy, verification, skills. 
(2) A Markdown summary (`intent_summary.md`) explaining your reasoning.
Do not output any extra text. Ensure all outputs are syntactically valid YAML/JSON. Use schema validation.
Schema Definitions: JSON schemas for expected fields (so the LLM’s JSON can be validated). This includes an Agent-Format schema, MCP connector list schema, etc. We embed these in the prompt (or as definitions:) for structured output.
Example Runs: In adapter/examples/, we show sample in-out pairs. For instance, given a brief on “build Slack bot that tracks orders and sends email notifications”, the adapter yields a complete .agf.yaml, a Conductor workflow, MCP tool lists (email, slack, database), and a table of key objectives/constraints.
Logic Code: A lightweight script (Python/Node) that takes the adapter output and generates the actual workflow and config files. It also cross-checks that all referenced files (like skill names) exist, and populates dynamic fields (timestamps, git commit).
By using this Adapter, users never write the complex wiring by hand. Any changes to project scope automatically regenerate the orchestration.

/examples/ – Sample Projects
